# COMPANION GUARD to the Dict/HashMap axis-fixtures for
# `collection_borrow_mut_writethrough`. `Set` and `HashSet` have NO
# subscript-assign (`s[k] = ()` is `E_NotIndexable`), so the same
# "mutate the collection through a `&`-param" intent lands via
# `.add()` — a METHOD call, which routes through
# `lower_method_call`'s receiver ptr-through path (a DIFFERENT
# compiler site from the one the sibling fixtures pin).
#
# AXIS PIN: Set[String] (kind OrderedSet) · `&`-param root ·
# `.add()` method-call receiver · `.len()` observation.
#
# NOT RED-VERIFIED against the sibling fix (which touches
# `lower_index_assign` only) — `.add()` already worked pre-fix on
# baseline `d00e71a0` (verified Round XXX Track G, printed 3/3).
# This fixture PINS the mutation-through-`&`-param class intent:
# a caller must see every `.add()` the callee made. If the method-
# call receiver path ever regresses in the same shape, `.len()` in
# main would show `0`. Per Core #12 authoring rule, a fixture that
# is not RED-verifiable against the specific fix under test states
# what it pins instead.
from std.collections import Set

void mutate(Set[String] &env):
    # env enters with "seed" already inserted by main (1 entry).
    env.add("a")
    env.add("b")
    env.add("c")
    print(env.len())      # 4  (seed + a + b + c)

void main():
    Set[String] s = Set[String]()
    s.add("seed")
    mutate(&s)
    print(s.len())        # 4
    print(s.contains("a"))
    print(s.contains("b"))
    print(s.contains("c"))
    print(s.contains("seed"))
